# LOCAL STATE -- READ THIS BEFORE ADDING AN EXCEPTION TO IT.
#
# `_state/` holds the PERSISTENT CHROME PROFILE (config.py: STATE_DIR, and
# PROFILE_DIR = STATE_DIR/chrome-profile). That profile is where the operator's
# LIVE LinkedIn SESSION COOKIES live: it is a working, signed-in login to his
# real account, not a cache that can be regenerated from public data. Anyone
# holding it is holding the account. Committing it would publish those cookies,
# and git does not forget -- deleting the files in a later commit leaves the
# credentials sitting in history for everyone who ever clones. Nothing under
# _state/ is ever committed, and no `!` un-ignore line belongs in this file.
_state/

# The profile directory again, unanchored, because LINKEDIN_PROFILE_DIR can
# relocate it out from under _state/ (config.py reads that env var). Same
# cookies, same rule, wherever inside the tree it lands.
chrome-profile/
browser_profile/

# Python
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Scratch
*.log

# Environment files -- never commit. Secrets land in .env by default, and a
# tracked .env means the next key added to it commits without anyone noticing.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Raw page captures taken from his live signed-in LinkedIn session.
# These are the INPUT to fixtures, never the fixture: full PII, live
# tracking tokens and urn:li ids. They sat untracked but UN-IGNORED while
# two agents swept unowned files into commits elsewhere in this project on
# the same day, which is one `git add -A` away from a leak. The scrubbed
# fixtures under tests/fixtures/ are the committed artefact; these are not.
_audit/_probe-*.html
_audit/*_raw.html
_audit/_fixture_sanitisation_check.txt
